UNIT canonical_string
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@bb8ded84fa50 cli/sir/canon.mjs, function canonicalString — the text every oracle hash (vectorSetSha) and every
  typed-value equality (eqCanonical) is computed over; SIR_SCHEMA §10 "The canonical string" states the same rule
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG canonical_string(value: json) -> text
ENVELOPE the SIG type is json because the closed type set has no recursive named type for the true domain, a CANONICAL
  TREE: text, bool, list of canonical trees, map[text, canonical tree] — no f64 and no none anywhere the rendering
  examines (numbers and none live inside the tree only as tagged maps such as {"t":"f64","bits":..} and {"t":"none"}).
  Every json value is IN the envelope; a json value that is not a canonical tree THROWS (R8), and that throw is part of
  the contract. Nesting is capped by R10 (a node deeper than 416 throws). The shared oracle carries values up to the
  codec's value depth of 100; the cap's boundary probes (raw depth 415..417) are deeper than that and are graded through
  the js extension only (js, ts); rust cannot receive them (its language file). The js file widens to host values beyond
  json, all of which throw except text holding unpaired surrogates (OUT-OF-DOMAIN).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Render a canonical tree as one compact, deterministic text: maps with their keys sorted, no whitespace outside
  text content, text quoted and escaped. The key "t" is reserved: a map tagged f64 renders as its bit pattern alone, a
  map tagged throw as its tag alone. Equal results mean canonically equal values; the result is what is hashed.
BEHAVIOR
R1 text -> QUOTE(text): a quotation mark, each character of the text rendered by TABLE ESCAPE in order, a quotation mark.
  bool -> "true" or "false".
R2 list -> "[" + the renderings of its elements, in order, separated by "," + "]". The empty list -> "[]".
R3 map -> "{" + one entry per key in KEY ORDER (R4), separated by ",", each entry QUOTE(key) + ":" + the rendering of
  its value + "}". The empty map -> "{}". Keys escape exactly as text (R1).
R4 KEY ORDER is ascending lexicographic comparison of the keys as sequences of 16-bit code units, a proper prefix
  sorting first; a character outside the basic multilingual plane counts as its two surrogate code units (lead unit in
  0xD800..0xDBFF). This is NOT scalar-value order: a key holding an astral character sorts BEFORE a key holding a
  character in 0xE000..0xFFFF at the same position ("😀" before "" before "￿"); both orders agree everywhere
  below 0xD800. Digit-spelled keys sort as text ("10" before "2"). CITED ANCHOR SIR_SCHEMA §10 — keys sort by 16-bit code units
R5 f64 PROJECTION. A map whose key "t" holds exactly the text "f64" must have key "bits" holding text of exactly 16
  characters, each a digit or a LOWER-case letter a..f; otherwise -> throws (absent, not text, 15 or 17 characters,
  upper-case hex). A valid one renders as "{\"bits\":" + QUOTE(bits) + ",\"t\":\"f64\"}". Every other entry, "v"
  included, is dropped WITHOUT being examined: a raw number or none under a dropped key does not throw. CITED FIDELITY package
R6 throw PROJECTION. A map whose key "t" holds exactly the text "throw" renders as "{\"t\":\"throw\"}"; every other
  entry, the message included, is dropped without being examined. CITED FIDELITY package
R7 The projections test only the exact text value of key "t" (case and whitespace significant; a list holding "f64" or a
  bool there does not match) and apply to EVERY map at every depth, inside lists and inside other maps' values. A list
  is never projected. Every other tag ("text", "map", "list", "i64", "none", "record", "bytes", ...) is an ordinary map
  under R3.
R8 A raw f64 (any value, NaN and -0 included) or a none reached anywhere by R1-R3 -> throws: the whole call throws and
  yields no text. That it throws is the observation; the message is a display hint, not part of the contract.
R9 The result contains no whitespace or separator outside quoted text: no space after "," or ":", no newline.
R10 NESTING CAP. Every node the rendering visits has a depth: the whole value is depth 0, and each element of a list and
  each value of a map is one deeper than the list or map holding it (keys are not nodes). Visiting a node deeper than
  416 -> throws; 416 = 4 x 100 + 16, the codec's 100-value cap at up to four raw levels per typed value, plus 16 (TABLE
  DIALS). Only visited nodes count: entries dropped by R5 and R6 are never visited at any depth, while the entries a
  projection renders ("bits" and "t") sit one level below the projected map. So a text or bool at depth 416 renders and at
  417 throws; an empty list or map at depth 416 renders and at 417 throws; an f64- or throw-tagged map at depth 416 throws,
  at 415 renders; a typed list chain {"t":"list","v":[...]} costs two depths per level. The check precedes every other rule
  at a node: a too-deep node throws whatever it holds. CITED FIDELITY package
TABLE DIALS — name -> value; meaning
  MAX_VALUE_DEPTH  -> 100; the codec's value-nesting cap (this unit never checks it; it sizes RAW_CAP)
  RAW_CAP          -> 416; 4 x MAX_VALUE_DEPTH + 16, the deepest node depth that renders
TABLE ESCAPE — one character of text (or of a key) -> its rendering; first matching row wins
  quotation mark                                       -> "\""
  reverse solidus                                      -> "\\"
  backspace 0x08                                       -> "\b"
  form feed 0x0C                                       -> "\f"
  line feed 0x0A                                       -> "\n"
  carriage return 0x0D                                 -> "\r"
  tab 0x09                                             -> "\t"
  any other control character 0x00..0x1F               -> "\u00" + two LOWER-case hex digits (0x1F -> "\u001f")
  an unpaired surrogate code unit (js widening only)   -> "\u" + four LOWER-case hex digits (0xD800 -> "\ud800")
  anything else, delete 0x7F, solidus "/", 0x2028, 0x2029 and every non-ASCII scalar included -> itself, unescaped
INVARIANT every non-throwing result is valid json text; parsed back it equals the input with the projections applied.
INVARIANT the result depends only on the value, never on the order in which a map's entries were built.
INVARIANT a json value containing no f64 and no none renders unless an f64-tagged map in it has invalid bits (R5).
OUT-OF-DOMAIN js widening (verified by the js and ts variants only; oracle vectors routed to the js extension). Throws, like
  R8: the host's absent value anywhere examined, a list hole, a big integer, and every host object that is not a plain
  map (keyed collections, regexps, dates, boxed primitives, typed arrays, class instances). Renders: text holding unpaired
  surrogates, escaped by TABLE ESCAPE; a map with no prototype, which is a map under R3. Also through the extension: the
  R10 boundary probes, deeper than the neutral codec's 100 value levels.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (no oracle vector; the codec cannot carry them): callables and symbols throw like R8; a key
  spelled "__proto__" is outside the oracle. Trees deeper than R10's cap are refused before any host stack is at risk: the
  real code renders a 416-deep chain on a default stack, and so must every artifact.
ORACLE value -> oracles/canonical_string.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 dd6f573b41c3d12d74b6fc1ba836ac41da345a96d75289581c4dcf46641bfbc5
SCOPE IN: R1-R9 over every json value, plus the js widening of OUT-OF-DOMAIN. OUT: eqCanonical, vectorSetSha and the
  encoder that builds typed-value trees (separate units calling this one), including the encoder's two map orders (§10 open item).
PROVENANCE rederive@bb8ded84fa50 (rederive, own code), cli/sir/canon.mjs, export canonicalString, driven through
  selfhost/seams/canonical_string.mjs; oracle stamped by selfhost/stamp.mjs from the pinned authority snapshot (24 frozen +
  48 held-out, seed 1). Round 1 re-cut 2026-10-26 after the round-0 findings narrowed the domain (rederive d7e1fb4); round 4
  re-cut 2026-10-27 for the nesting cap R10 (selfhost FINDINGS 66); round 5 re-cut 2026-10-27 for RAW_CAP 416 (FINDINGS
  78), the cap probes graded through the js extension. The independent Python implementation, sir-lab
  lib/canon.py canonical_string, agrees with every vector and with R10.
